{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implementation note — one deliberate deviation from the certified plan, strengthening a test.\n\nThe plan specified the `_LLM_KNOWN_KEYS` sync test as `set(_LLM_KNOWN_KEYS) - {\"evals\"} == collected`, with `\"evals\" in _LLM_KNOWN_KEYS` asserted separately, on the assumption that `evals` is not AST-collectible because `_evals_corpus_root` builds a synthetic flattened `evals.corpus_root` key rather than reading `evals` directly.\n\nThat assumption is wrong. `evals` IS collectible: `config_readers.py` reaches it via `_read_llm_file_table(repo_root).get(\"evals\")`, which is one of the two reader shapes the extractor already recognises. The implemented test therefore asserts plain set equality in both directions with no carve-out. Same intent, strictly stronger: an enrolled key that no reader reads now fails, where the carve-out form would have let `evals` drift unchecked.\n\nSecond correction found the same way. The plan said the `[llm]` inventory is \"the 15 keys read in `config_readers.py` plus the three `llm/` modules, plus `evals`\" — i.e. 16. AST-walking the readers rather than trusting the count gives **15** (14 non-`evals` + `evals`). `_LLM_KNOWN_KEYS` holds 15. The sync test pins the real number, so the registry self-corrects rather than inheriting the plan's arithmetic.\n\nScope and behaviour are otherwise exactly as certified. Complexity arithmetic landed on the plan's prediction: `coerce_sparse` 15 → 12 after the alias loop came out → 13 with the `[llm]` branch, held to +1 by extracting `_warn_unknown_llm_keys`. Both ratchets are clean (`complexity: new=0 increased=0`; `mechanism: new=0`).\n\nSweep result worth recording: 15 files mention the 13 retired warn-class names, and **zero** outside the 3 in-scope test files needed editing. The references are `monkeypatch.delenv` env-hygiene lists, assertions that `REBAR_PUSH` is *ignored* (still true), assertions that the names are *absent* from generated docs (still true), and historical narrative in `docs/bash-migration.md`. What a reference asserts matters more than that it matches the grep."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793426968865688489,"uuid":"61004747-5f02-4095-8339-a6b852261297"}